{
       "name": "Target-Fallback LBD Phase Selection",
       "algorithm": "Step 1: Maintain two independent phase-saving arrays, `phase_focused[v]` and `phase_stable[v]`. During backtracking, update only the array corresponding to the solver's current search mode (focused/exploration vs. stable/exploitation). Step 2: Maintain a phase quality matrix `phase_score[v][2]` (tracking scores for True and False assignments). During conflict analysis, for each variable involved in the conflict, add `1.0 / LBD` to `phase_score[v][assigned_phase]`. Decay all scores by a factor of 0.95 at every restart. Step 3: Inside `kissat_decide_phase(v)`, identify the current search mode and retrieve the baseline candidate phase: `P = phase_focused[v]` if in focused mode, otherwise `P = phase_stable[v]`. Step 4: Evaluate the historical LBD quality of the candidate phase. If the opposite phase has a significantly better track record—specifically, if `phase_score[v][!P] > 1.5 * phase_score[v][P]` AND `phase_score[v][!P] > 2.0` (minimum confidence threshold)—override the baseline and select `!P`. Step 5: If the override conditions in Step 4 are not met, evaluate the absolute confidence of the baseline candidate `P`. If both `phase_score[v][P] < 0.5` and `phase_score[v][!P] < 0.5` (indicating a lack of recent participation in high-quality learned clauses for this variable), return Kissat's globally saved target phase (the phase from the historically longest conflict-free assignment trail). Otherwise, return the baseline candidate phase `P`.",
       "reason": "In Step 5 of the original algorithm, the solver blindly defaults back to the baseline candidate phase `P` when the override conditions fail, even if neither phase has yielded good recent LBDs. This variant improves upon the original by recognizing when local conflict data is weak or "stale" (scores < 0.5). By falling back to Kissat's target phase in these low-confidence scenarios, the heuristic effectively bridges local conflict-driven phase selection with global trail-based phase selection. This biases the search toward globally promising, satisfiable regions when local guidance is uninformative, significantly reducing timeouts on satisfiable instances and improving the overall PAR2 score."
}

